\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>Network monitoring is the practice of continuously observing a network to detect failing or slow components. It matters because proactive visibility minimizes downtime and degrades gracefully instead of failing catastrophically.</p>
            
            <h3>What to Monitor</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Availability:</strong> Is the device online? Are specific service checks (like HTTP) responding?</li>
                <li><strong>Performance:</strong> What is the <a href="network-latency-troubleshooting.html">latency</a> or <a href="packet-loss-troubleshooting.html">packet loss</a> across critical paths?</li>
                <li><strong>Capacity:</strong> Are interfaces approaching bandwidth limits? Are device CPU, memory, or hardware sensors (temperature) exceeding thresholds?</li>
                <li><strong>Errors:</strong> Are interfaces silently dropping packets or recording CRC errors?</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Monitoring Concepts</h3>
            <p>Visibility is achieved through combinations of <strong>active checks</strong> (synthetic ping/HTTP requests), <strong>polling</strong> (pulling stats via <a href="snmp-explained.html">SNMP</a> or APIs), and <strong>passive data</strong> (receiving Syslog events, SNMP traps, or flow telemetry like NetFlow).</p>
            <p>Modern tools use this data to establish <strong>baselines</strong> (normal historical behavior), identify <strong>trending</strong> patterns, and trigger <strong>alerting</strong> when metrics cross defined <strong>thresholds</strong>.</p>
            
            <h3>Troubleshooting Context</h3>
            <p>An alert is evidence of a condition, not automatically proof of root cause. A high-CPU alert on a router might be normal during a routing table recalculation, or it might be a denial-of-service attack.</p>
            
            <h4>Example Workflow</h4>
            <pre><code>Monitor detects WAN packet loss
       ↓
Verify router interface counters (check for physical errors)
       ↓
Ping local gateway
       ↓
Ping ISP next hop
       ↓
Compare latency/loss at each step
       ↓
Check interface bandwidth utilization
       ↓
Escalate with gathered evidence</code></pre>
        
        </div>
        
